          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "Based on what you've described, the screening tool found no emergency pattern. That is not a guarantee of safety. The tool only checks a fixed list of warning signs. Seek urgent care now if you have chest pain, breathlessness, fainting, a severe headache, vision changes, weakness or numbness on one side, confusion, or trouble speaking. The emergency notes in this system list chest pain, breathlessness at rest, and fainting as reasons for urgent assessment (Iron-deficiency anemia reference).\n\nThe guidance describes how blood pressure is confirmed and treated. A diagnosis uses an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions, and home readings are one accepted way to take them (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets). For most treated adults, US guidance describes a target below 130/80 mm Hg (same document). Follow-up is described as monthly review until the target is reached, then every three to six months once stable. Reviews include checking adherence and, after starting certain blood-pressure medicines, kidney function and potassium (Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line). The same guidance says apparent poor control is often due to measurement, adherence, or medicines that raise blood pressure, such as some over-the-counter painkillers and decongestants (same document).\n\nThe sources do not say what a reading of 155/95 means for urgency, and they do not describe what happens after stopping blood-pressure medication. Please don't restart or change any medicine on your own. Contact the clinician who prescribed it, tell them about the stopped tablets and your home readings, and ask whether treatment should resume and how often you should be checked.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
